## 3. Important Code & Deep Dive

### 3.1 The Submission State Machine (`submission.service.ts`)
```typescript
const VALID_TRANSITIONS: Record<SubmissionStatus, SubmissionStatus[]> = {
  [SubmissionStatus.QUEUED]: [
    SubmissionStatus.RUNNING,
    SubmissionStatus.SYSTEM_ERROR,
  ],
  [SubmissionStatus.RUNNING]: [
    SubmissionStatus.ACCEPTED,
    SubmissionStatus.WRONG_ANSWER,
    SubmissionStatus.TIME_LIMIT_EXCEEDED,
    SubmissionStatus.MEMORY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED,
    SubmissionStatus.COMPILATION_ERROR,
    SubmissionStatus.RUNTIME_ERROR,
    SubmissionStatus.SYSTEM_ERROR,
  ],
  // Terminal states cannot transition to anything
  [SubmissionStatus.ACCEPTED]: [],
  [SubmissionStatus.WRONG_ANSWER]: [],
  [SubmissionStatus.TIME_LIMIT_EXCEEDED]: [],
  [SubmissionStatus.MEMORY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED]: [],
  [SubmissionStatus.COMPILATION_ERROR]: [],
  [SubmissionStatus.RUNTIME_ERROR]: [],
  [SubmissionStatus.SYSTEM_ERROR]: [],
};
```
*Why this matters:* In a high-concurrency system with retries, workers, or duplicate requests, state machine guards guarantee that a finished submission can never be moved back into `RUNNING` or `QUEUED`.

### 3.2 Output Normalization & Comparison (`BaseExecutor.ts`)
```typescript
public compareOutput(actual: string, expected: string): boolean {
  return this.normalizeOutput(actual) === this.normalizeOutput(expected);
}

protected normalizeOutput(output: string): string {
  return output
    .replace(/\r\n/g, '\n')       // Convert Windows line breaks to Unix
    .split('\n')
    .map((line) => line.trimEnd()) // Remove trailing spaces on lines
    .join('\n')
    .trim();                       // Remove trailing blank lines
}
```
*Why this matters:* Different operating systems and compilers produce subtle differences in line endings (`\r\n` on Windows vs `\n` on Linux) and trailing spaces. Without normalization, correct solutions would falsely fail due to invisible whitespace differences.

### 3.3 Safe Process Spawning with Timeout Guard (`BaseExecutor.ts`)
```typescript
const timer = setTimeout(() => {
  timedOut = true;
  child.kill('SIGKILL');
}, timeLimitMs);
```
*Why this matters:* If a user submits `while True: pass`, a synchronous or unguarded process would run indefinitely, consume 100% of a CPU core, and starve the server. The timer guarantees that the child process is terminated if it exceeds `timeLimitMs`, returning `TIME_LIMIT_EXCEEDED`.

